
-- Batch 4: More African (Nigeria continued through Zimbabwe) + Rwanda/Tanzania/Uganda etc
UPDATE recipes SET continent = 'Africa', history = 'Isombe is Rwanda''s national dish, made from mashed cassava leaves cooked with eggplant, spinach, and palm oil. This nutrient-dense dish represents the agricultural traditions of the Great Lakes region. Rwandan cuisine emphasizes simplicity and nourishment, and isombe is central to family meals and celebrations.' WHERE slug = 'isombe-rwanda';

UPDATE recipes SET continent = 'Africa', history = 'Thieboudienne Poulet is a Senegalese variation of the classic thieboudienne, using chicken instead of fish. Senegalese cuisine, recognized by UNESCO, blends Wolof, French, and Lebanese influences. This dish reflects the inland regions where freshwater fish is less available but poultry is abundant.' WHERE slug = 'thieboudienne-poulet-senegal';

UPDATE recipes SET continent = 'Africa', history = 'Cassava Leaf Stew is Sierra Leone''s most popular dish, a hearty stew of pounded cassava leaves cooked with palm oil, fish, and meat. The dish represents the agricultural backbone of Sierra Leonean cuisine. Cassava, brought from South America centuries ago, became the country''s most important crop.' WHERE slug = 'cassava-leaf-stew-sierra-leone';

UPDATE recipes SET continent = 'Africa', history = 'Canjeero (Laxoox) is Somalia''s version of the pan-African pancake-like flatbread, similar to Ethiopian injera but thinner. Made from fermented batter, canjeero is a breakfast staple served with honey, ghee, or stew. It represents the pastoral and agricultural traditions of Somali cuisine.' WHERE slug = 'canjeero-somalia';

UPDATE recipes SET continent = 'Africa', history = 'Biltong Snack Bites are a South African dried meat delicacy dating back to the 17th century Dutch settlers who needed to preserve meat in the Cape. The word comes from Dutch "bil" (rump) and "tong" (strip). Today, biltong is a national obsession and a distinct South African contribution to world cuisine.' WHERE slug = 'biltong-snack-bites-za';

UPDATE recipes SET continent = 'Africa', history = 'Boerewors Rolls are South Africa''s beloved street food, featuring the iconic spiral sausage in a bread roll. Boerewors ("farmer''s sausage") has Afrikaner origins dating to the 17th century Cape Colony. The recipe, protected by South African law, must contain at least 90% meat. It''s essential at every braai (BBQ).' WHERE slug = 'boerewors-rolls-za';

UPDATE recipes SET continent = 'Africa', history = 'Koeksister is a traditional South African deep-fried dough pastry braided and soaked in cold sugar syrup. Of Afrikaner origin, it dates to the 18th century Cape Colony. The name comes from Dutch "koek" (cake). The syrupy, crunchy exterior and soft interior make it an irresistible treat at every South African gathering.' WHERE slug = 'koeksister-za';

UPDATE recipes SET continent = 'Africa', history = 'Melktert (Milk Tart) is a beloved South African dessert with roots in Dutch colonial baking traditions. This custard-filled tart dusted with cinnamon has been a staple of Cape Malay and Afrikaner cuisine since the 17th century. It''s a comfort food that evokes nostalgia across all South African communities.' WHERE slug = 'melktert-za';

UPDATE recipes SET continent = 'Africa', history = 'Pap and Chakalaka is South Africa''s iconic combination of maize porridge with a spicy vegetable relish. Chakalaka was created in the townships of Johannesburg by mine workers who improvised with available ingredients. The dish represents the resilience and creativity of South African urban cooking.' WHERE slug = 'pap-chakalaka-za';

UPDATE recipes SET continent = 'Africa', history = 'Potjiekos is a traditional South African stew slow-cooked in a three-legged cast-iron pot over an open fire. The tradition dates back to the Dutch settlers and Voortrekkers of the 17th-18th centuries. The name means "small pot food" and the layered cooking method creates complex, deeply satisfying flavors.' WHERE slug = 'potjiekos-za';

UPDATE recipes SET continent = 'Africa', history = 'South African Braai is more than just a barbecue—it''s a national ritual and cultural institution. Heritage Day on September 24th is unofficially known as "National Braai Day." The tradition spans all South African communities, from Afrikaner boerewors braais to Zulu and Xhosa meat celebrations.' WHERE slug = 'south-african-braai';

UPDATE recipes SET continent = 'Africa', history = 'Vetkoek is a traditional South African deep-fried bread that traces its origins to the Dutch settlers'' "vetkoek" (fat cake). These golden, crispy-outside-soft-inside breads are filled with savory mince, cheese, or jam. Found at every market and home kitchen, vetkoek is beloved comfort food across South Africa.' WHERE slug = 'vetkoek-za';

UPDATE recipes SET continent = 'Africa', history = 'Ful Medames is Sudan''s beloved fava bean stew, shared across North and East Africa. In Sudan, ful is the national breakfast, enjoyed with bread and accompanied by cheese, eggs, and salad. The dish connects Sudanese cuisine to its ancient Egyptian roots, where fava beans have been cultivated for millennia.' WHERE slug = 'ful-medames-sudan';

UPDATE recipes SET continent = 'Africa', history = 'Kachumbari is a fresh tomato and onion salad ubiquitous across Tanzania and East Africa. This simple yet essential accompaniment to grilled meats and stews represents the fresh, vibrant flavors of East African cuisine. The dish has roots in Indian culinary influence along the Swahili Coast.' WHERE slug = 'kachumbari-tanzania';

UPDATE recipes SET continent = 'Africa', history = 'Tanzanian Chips Mayai is a popular Tanzanian street food—an omelet loaded with french fries. This unique fusion dish reflects the creative street food culture of Dar es Salaam and represents how Tanzanian vendors transform simple ingredients into satisfying, affordable meals for workers and students.' WHERE slug = 'chips-mayai-tanzania';

UPDATE recipes SET continent = 'Africa', history = 'Zanzibar Pizza is a unique street food creation from the night markets of Stone Town, Zanzibar. Despite its name, it bears little resemblance to Italian pizza—it''s a thin crepe-like pastry filled with meat, vegetables, cheese, and sometimes Nutella. It represents Zanzibar''s creative fusion of African, Arab, and Indian cuisines.' WHERE slug = 'zanzibar-pizza-tanzania';

UPDATE recipes SET continent = 'Africa', history = 'Djenkoumé is a traditional Togolese dish of corn dough cooked in a spiced tomato sauce. This dish is a staple across Togo and reflects the importance of corn in West African diets since its introduction from the Americas. It showcases the Togolese ability to create rich, satisfying meals from simple ingredients.' WHERE slug = 'djenkoume-togo';

UPDATE recipes SET continent = 'Africa', history = 'Lablabi is a Tunisian chickpea soup that is the country''s most popular street breakfast. Served in bowls with torn bread, harissa, olive oil, and cumin, lablabi represents Tunisia''s Mediterranean and Berber culinary heritage. This hearty, affordable dish has sustained Tunisian workers and students for generations.' WHERE slug = 'lablabi-tunisia';

UPDATE recipes SET continent = 'Africa', history = 'Rolex is Uganda''s iconic street food—a chapati rolled around a fried egg omelet with vegetables. The name is a playful abbreviation of "rolled eggs." Created in the early 2000s in Kampala, the Rolex has become Uganda''s most famous culinary invention and represents the vibrant creativity of East African street food culture.' WHERE slug = 'rolex-uganda';

UPDATE recipes SET continent = 'Africa', history = 'Nshima is Zambia''s staple food, a thick maize porridge that forms the foundation of every meal. Similar to other Southern African maize staples, nshima is eaten with relish (ndiwo) of vegetables, fish, or meat. The communal act of eating nshima from a shared plate reinforces Zambian family bonds and hospitality.' WHERE slug = 'nshima-zambia';

UPDATE recipes SET continent = 'Africa', history = 'Sadza is Zimbabwe''s national staple, a thick maize porridge that is the cornerstone of Shona and Ndebele cuisine. Eaten with every meal, sadza represents the agricultural traditions and cultural identity of Zimbabwe. The communal preparation and eating of sadza reinforces family bonds and Zimbabwean hospitality.' WHERE slug = 'sadza-zimbabwe';

UPDATE recipes SET continent = 'Africa', history = 'Mshikaki are East African beef kebabs popular in Tanzania. These marinated, grilled meat skewers are a beloved street food with roots in the culinary traditions of the Swahili Coast, blending Arab, Indian, and African flavoring techniques. Mshikaki vendors are a common sight in every Tanzanian town.' WHERE slug = 'mshikaki-tanzania';

UPDATE recipes SET continent = 'Africa', history = 'Swahili Biryani reflects the rich maritime trade history of the East African coast. Brought by Arab, Indian, and Persian traders who sailed to ports like Mombasa and Zanzibar, biryani was adapted with local spices and cooking techniques. Swahili biryani is a festive dish served at weddings and Eid celebrations.' WHERE slug = 'swahili-biryani';

UPDATE recipes SET continent = 'Africa', history = 'Suya is Nigeria''s most iconic street food, spiced grilled meat skewers originating from the Hausa people of northern Nigeria. The distinctive yaji spice blend, made from ground peanuts, ginger, paprika, and cayenne pepper, gives suya its unique flavor. Suya spots across West Africa are social gathering places.' WHERE slug = 'suya-ng';

UPDATE recipes SET continent = 'Africa', history = 'Mandazi are sweet, lightly spiced East African doughnuts popular across Kenya, Tanzania, and Uganda. Often flavored with cardamom and coconut milk, these triangle or round-shaped treats are a beloved breakfast and tea-time snack. Mandazi reflects the Indian Ocean trade routes that brought spices to East Africa.' WHERE slug = 'mandazi-ke';
